# Python-generated files
__pycache__/
*.py[oc]
build/
dist/
wheels/
*.egg-info

# Tool caches. Here rather than only in a personal global ignore file, which is
# where they were being caught — so they were untracked-and-invisible on this
# machine and untracked-and-noisy on any fresh clone.
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# sooper seekrit
scratch/

# Virtual environments
.venv

# Derived rasters. `scans/` is exactly `pdftoppm -r <dpi>` over `pdfs/` —
# verified pixel-identical — so it is regenerable, not source. Keeping it out
# matters: it is 212 MB at 150 dpi and grows with every re-render, and git
# would keep each generation forever.
scans/

# Downloaded model weights (675 MB), not authored here.
#
# `models/*` rather than `models/`, and that is not a style choice: git does not
# descend into an excluded *directory*, so a `!models/bogoras-seg/` negation under
# `models/` never fires. Excluding the contents instead leaves the directory
# readable and the negation working.
models/*
# — except the models this project trains, which are small, ours, and meaningless
# without the corpus they were fitted to. Each carries the split it trained on as
# `<model>.train.lst` / `.eval.lst` — verbatim copies of what `--assemble` wrote,
# not a re-encoding — plus a small `<model>.json` for the few facts that are not
# lists: a digest of `gt/zones/` at the time, the merges in force, and the score.
# Paths in it are repo-relative, since a record of one machine's filesystem is no
# record. `.bak` is `--remap-model`'s one-deep undo and stays out, like
# `gt/zones/*.prev`.
!models/bogoras-seg/
models/bogoras-seg/*.bak
# The figure segmenter, on the same terms. Its manifest's corpus digest is over
# `gt/fig/` rather than `gt/zones/`, which is the whole reason the two are separate
# models rather than one with more classes: they are fitted to different stores and
# read different rasters — zones-seg trains on figure-blotted pages, and a figure
# segmenter obviously cannot, since the blot paints out its target.
!models/bogoras-fig/
models/bogoras-fig/*.bak

# Transient working dirs: recognition output kept only for comparison, and
# the sxiv review gates.
ocr300/
figs-ocr300/

# `review/PP-NNN/` is one directory per page, holding both the authored files —
# `proposal.json` (somebody's, or something's, reading) and `decision.json` (an
# editorial act) — and the derived images, which are regenerable from the ALTO and
# the rasters. Losing an authored one is the failure this repo already suffered once.
#
# So the derived names are listed and everything else is tracked, which is the
# reverse of the earlier `review/*` + negate-two-directories rule, deliberately. Under
# that rule a *new* authored file needed a matching negation or it was silently
# ignored, i.e. untracked and losable — the exact failure mode. Under this one a
# forgotten entry commits a few PNGs: noise, and visible. `harness/paths.py:DERIVED`
# is the list these mirror; keep the two in step.
review/*/crops/
review/*/sheets/
review/*/zoom/
review/*/review.html
# The whole page with its line indices drawn in a left gutter — the reading pass's
# first image, and derived like the rest. `review/*/*.png` below would cover it, but
# that pattern exists for the `page.png` symlink and is deliberately not read as a
# DERIVED entry (see test_layout.py), so the name is listed here explicitly.
review/*/numbered.png
# The save writes temp-then-rename and keeps one .prev alongside, as insurance
# against an interrupted write, plus an append-only .jsonl journal of every
# save for mid-session incident recovery (the Firefox session-restore bug
# review.py's docstring describes). None of the three belongs in history —
# they're forensic/recovery data for *before* a commit happens, not authored
# content, and git already is the durable record once one does.
review/*/*.prev
review/*/*.tmp
review/*/*.jsonl
# `page.png` is a symlink to the page's raster, put there so opening a page's own
# directory shows the page. Same disposition as the three above — ignored, and NOT
# in `DERIVED`, so `harness clean` leaves it once the images are gone. It cannot be
# tracked: `scans/` is derived and ignored, so a committed link would dangle in
# every clone, exactly as `scans/w-figs/` would.
review/*/*.png
# The dispatch queue and the claims taken off it. Not `review/*/…` and so not
# `DERIVED`'s business (test_layout.py holds that pair over the per-page names
# alone): these are the batch's working state, live only while readers are out,
# and cleared by the next `ocr queue`. A claim is a statement that one agent took
# one page in one run — committing it would deal that page to nobody in the next
# clone. `harness/paths.py:page_dirs` is what stops the sweeps reading them as
# pages.
review/_queue.txt
review/_claims/
# The collation pass's pair, same argument. They live beside `tei/` because that is
# the store this batch deals out, exactly as the two above live beside `review/`.
# These two used to be the only holes in an otherwise fully tracked `tei/`; the
# temporary `/tei/chukchi-*.xml` entry below is a third and much larger one, and
# unlike these it is meant to be removed.
tei/_queue.txt
tei/_claims/

# Symlink farms pointing *into* `scans/`, which is ignored above — so committing
# them yields 272 dangling links on a fresh clone. Regenerable from the `fig/` and
# `ocr/` listings, i.e. derived by the same argument that keeps `scans/` out.
#
# `ocr/` is deliberately NOT here, and the reason is worth stating in full since
# half of it used to be missing. Its symlinks resolve *inside* the repo, into
# `gt/ocr/`, so a clone gets working links rather than dangling ones — that is the
# half that was already written down. The other half is what `ocr/` is *for*: it is
# the read view over every OCR store, the stable name by which a page's current OCR
# is reached whatever produced it, and `harness`'s default `--from`
# (`lines.DEFAULT_ALTO_DIR`). Raw recognizer output, if any is produced again, links
# in beside the ground truth from its own real store rather than displacing it — so
# a file's rank stays readable from where its link points, which is precisely the
# property whose absence caused the ground-truth loss.
scans/w-figs/
scans/w-ocr/
# `gt/zones/` is tracked — the page ALTOs, the three split lists, `_rule_exempt.json`
# and `_ink_exempt.json`
# are the authored record. What is ignored is the recovery data beside them, the
# same third disposition `review/*/` already carries: `.prev` is one save deep and
# `_journal.jsonl` is the append-only log that survives the two saves which destroy
# it. Both matter only before a commit exists, which is exactly when git cannot
# help. Same reasoning as `review/*/*.prev` above; see harness/zones.py.
gt/zones/*.prev
gt/zones/*.tmp
gt/zones/_journal.jsonl
# `gt/fig/` on exactly the same terms: the 252 page ALTOs, the two split lists and
# `_rule_exempt.json` are the authored record; the recovery data beside them is not.
# `METS.xml` joins them because it is *derived from the store it sits in* — the one
# that used to be tracked here was an eScriptorium export artifact describing a
# store that has since been rewritten under it, which is a second copy of the
# store's shape with nothing keeping the two honest. `harness fig mets --out DIR`
# builds one on demand, and requires the directory so it cannot land back here.
gt/fig/*.prev
gt/fig/*.tmp
gt/fig/_journal.jsonl
gt/fig/METS.xml
# `gt/lines/` is tracked on the same terms — the correction fragments and
# `_ink_exempt.json` are the authored record, the recovery copy beside them is
# not. Note what is *not* ignored: `gt/lines/*.xml` is a human's geometry over
# ink the segmenter missed, and it exists nowhere else. There is no journal here
# because the store has no history to keep that the file itself does not — the
# fragment is the whole record of the act.
gt/lines/*.prev
gt/lines/*.tmp
# Derived, and disposable in a stronger sense than the two above: the uncovered-ink
# verdict per page, written by whatever last read the raster, stamped with the
# mtimes it was true for. Deleting it costs one re-check; committing it would put a
# claim about a page's ink in history with nothing to hold it accountable.
gt/zones/_ink_cache.json
# `harness ocr blot PP-NNN`: each page with every line box and figure zone
# painted out, so whatever is left is uncovered by construction. Derived from
# `scans/` and the ALTO stores, regenerable, and one PNG per page across 832 pages
# — nothing here belongs in history.
#
# **Both blot trees are under `scans/`, so both are already covered by `scans/` at
# the top of this file.** They are spelled out anyway, for the reason the two farms
# below them are: the entry is where the rationale lives, and a derived tree with no
# line here reads as one nobody thought about. They moved under `scans/` from the
# repo root because every other scan-derived tree was already there, and a rename
# that left one of a pair behind would manufacture exactly the near-miss naming this
# repo keeps paying for.
scans/w-blotted/
# `harness zones blot`: the segmenter's training input — each page with its
# figure zones painted **white**, so a paragraph wrapping around an illustration
# cannot shape what the model learns about zone geometry. Distinct from
# `scans/w-blotted/` above, which paints in conspicuous colours for a human to read;
# this one has to look like blank paper. Derived and rebuilt in one command, and
# the 580 figure-less pages here are symlinks into the already-ignored `scans/`,
# so a tracked copy would dangle in every clone exactly as `scans/w-figs/` would.
scans/w-figs-blotted/
# `harness fig crops`: one PNG per declared `GraphicZone`, cut out of `scans/` for
# the edition and whatever eventually builds an epub. 414 files, every one of them
# a rectangle already recorded in `gt/fig/` applied to a raster that is itself
# derived — so there is nothing here a clone cannot rebuild in one command, and a
# tracked copy would be 414 pictures of a book in a repo that deliberately carries
# none. Covered by `scans/` above and spelled out for the reason its neighbours
# are: the entry is where the rationale lives.
#
# **`gt/fig-img/` is the opposite and is deliberately absent from this file.** A
# picture somebody touched up by hand is authored work that exists nowhere else,
# so it is tracked, and it lives under `gt/` because that is what `gt/` means. The
# split is the whole safety property: a rebuild rewrites everything here and
# nothing there, and `figures.crop_source` resolves the human file first.
scans/fig-crops/
# Training scratch: `--assemble`'s symlink farm, its two .lst files, and whatever
# checkpoints a run writes. **Pure scratch, and that is a property worth keeping** —
# the split a model trained on travels in that model's own manifest under
# `models/bogoras-seg/`, so nothing here is read once training ends and `--assemble`
# may clobber it freely. It used to be both the input and the only record of a past
# run, which meant preparing the next run destroyed the ability to interpret the
# model you already had.
#
# `bogoras-rec/` and `log/` live here for the same reason and used to be ignored by
# name at the repo root. Only a *trained* model is worth keeping and those live in
# `models/`; a run's mid-training checkpoints — `checkpoint_abort.ckpt` among them —
# have no consumer at all now that recognition fine-tuning is off the plan
# (CLAUDE.md, "Recognition").
train/
# What the models emitted, one raw ALTO per page: `harness zones predict` writes the
# first, `harness ocr predict` the second. Derived in the strong sense — regenerable
# from the checkpoints in `models/` and the rasters, all of which are kept — and
# neither is a record of anybody's judgement, which is the property that decides
# this. A prediction a human has been through stops living here: it is corrected in
# the editor and saved into the tracked `gt/`, so what is worth keeping is kept by
# being promoted.
/mz/zones/
/mz/ocr/
# `mz/fig/` too, and note there is deliberately **no `fig/` view** over it and
# `gt/fig/` the way `zones/` covers the zone stores: every consumer of figure
# geometry names `gt/fig` directly, and the one thing a view would buy is the one
# thing that must not happen — `zones blot` painting the training rasters from
# machine figure claims and the zone segmenter then learning them.
/mz/fig/
# `mz/tei/` is the fourth, and the odd one: not a model's output but a *rule's*.
# `harness tei generate` reads `gt/zones/` and `gt/ocr/` — both human-adjudicated —
# and folds them into a first-draft TEI page, so its content is derived in the
# strongest sense available here: same inputs, same bytes, no checkpoint and no
# sampling in between. Regenerating it costs a second.
#
# It is ignored anyway, and for the reason that decides all four: **it is nobody's
# judgement.** What a human ruled is already in the two stores it reads, and what
# the collator ruled is in `tei/`, which is tracked. A committed copy would be a
# third place the same facts live, going stale against both.
#
# **There is deliberately no view over the tei pair**, as with `fig` and unlike
# `ocr`/`zones`. A view exists to give one stable name to a page's current best
# reading; here the two files mean *different things* — `mz/tei/` is what the rules
# produced and `tei/` is what a reader signed — and `harness tei diff` is the whole
# point of being able to name them apart. A view would make `tei/…` ambiguous
# between them, which is the one property this pair must not have.
#
# Anchored, like its siblings: a bare `tei/` would swallow the tracked `tei/` at the
# repo root, and a store silently untracked is the failure this repo has already
# paid for once.
/mz/tei/
# **TEMPORARY, and the only entry in this file that is.** The collated pages are
# out of history while the generator is being fixed: `tei generate` is going to be
# rewritten repeatedly — figures anchored by default, footnote parsing, consecutive
# `MainZone-Continued` — and every rewrite invalidates every page downstream of it,
# so what is in `tei/` right now is a draft against a generator nobody trusts yet.
# Committing that is committing churn, not judgement.
#
# **Delete this entry the moment the generator settles**, and re-add the store. A
# collated page IS somebody's judgement — the one property that decides every other
# line in this file — so this is a store that belongs in history and is being kept
# out of it on purpose, for a stated and expiring reason. The longer this sits the
# more it looks like a rule.
#
# Named by pattern rather than `/tei/*` with a negation for `corpus-header.xml`, for
# the reason `review/` spells out above: under a negation rule a *new* authored file
# in `tei/` is silently ignored unless somebody remembers to negate it too, which is
# the losable-file failure in a different costume. This way a new authored name shows
# up in `git status` and only the page files are dropped.
/tei/chukchi-*.xml
# The two read views, one name per page, the human file winning:
# `harness views build zones` builds the first, `views build ocr` the second.
#
# **`ocr/` was tracked until `ocr predict` existed, and that is exactly why it is not
# any more.** Its links used to resolve inside tracked `gt/ocr/`, so a clone got
# working links and committing them cost nothing. Now half the farm points into the
# ignored `/mz/ocr/` above, so a committed copy would dangle on clone exactly as
# `scans/w-figs/` would — the same argument that has always kept `zones/` out, become
# true of `ocr/` the moment it acquired a second store to resolve into. Both are
# rebuilt in one command, and the commit that ignored `ocr/` is where its 20 tracked
# links were removed.
#
# **Both patterns are anchored with a leading slash, and that is not tidiness.** A
# bare `zones/` is a match on any directory of that name at any depth, so it silently
# swallows the *tracked* `gt/zones/` — a newly keyed page would then never be added,
# and `git status` would say nothing, which is the failure this repo has already paid
# for once in a different costume. `ocr/` is the sharper case of the same trap: a bare
# pattern would swallow `gt/ocr/`, the ground-truth store this project has already
# lost once. Verified with `git check-ignore -v`.
/zones/
/ocr/

# Agent worktrees — real git worktrees of this repo, never content of it.
.claude/worktrees/

# **`gt/page-img/` is tracked and is deliberately absent from this file**, on
# `gt/fig-img/`'s terms one level up: an editor's own version of a whole leaf is
# authored work that exists nowhere else. It was ignored for a while, pending the
# format — a vectorization of `1-361` was being attempted and committing a 22 MB
# raster about to be superseded would have put the wrong thing in git permanently.
# The vector attempt is dead (`tools/build_leaf_layers.py` records why) and the
# master is settled, so the entry came out. A vouched-for artefact a clone does not
# get is not vouched for.
#
# What is derived from it is the two display layers, and they are ignored here for
# `/mz/tei/`'s reason exactly: a rule's output over a tracked store, rebuilt in one
# command, and nobody's judgement. `tools/build_leaf_layers.py` writes them.
/mz/page-img/
